{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED for exact head bb170cf2f0bb8a2d2ecc945824231b6d57faca7a, naming artifact 9f57f3172abbb1a3a73cffadd9a2a3c7795cf043 at notes/2026-08-11-conversation-model.md.\n\nThe model is coherent and its fidelity to Winograd and Flores is high. The four-act backbone is right, the counteroffer path is right, and declining completion returning the same conversation to performance — rather than opening a successor assignment — is the correct reading and the one that fixes the failure that prompted this. Three things stand between it and adoption, all additive and cheap.\n\nWHAT I CHECKED FIRST, so the review is about the model rather than its packaging. The head is divergent, not a fast-forward: one commit on a3d097dd while main is fifteen ahead, including the documentation merge and today's fifteen retirements. That turns out not to matter — the note adds one new file, all four lineage notes it cites exist at main, and internal/docset scopes the citation gates to DocsDir = \"docs\", so notes/ is ungated and the absent rests_on front matter is correct rather than a defect. I checked that rather than assuming it.\n\n1. RECONCILE WITH AGENTS.md, WHICH CURRENTLY PRESCRIBES OTHERWISE. Two acts in the ontology table already have prescribed expressions in the governing document, and the note does not mention either. AGENTS.md lines 396 to 398 say to record an `assert` resting on the promise when a blockage is material, and to supersede a promise only when withdrawing it. The note introduces `interim report` and `withdraw promise` as separate acts without saying whether they replace that guidance or are satisfied by it.\n\nFor `interim report` this costs one sentence: the note already allows \"separate kinds or a declared subtype or phase field\", and `assert` resting on the promise satisfies that today. Naming it stops an implementer growing the vocabulary for something the log can already express.\n\nFor `withdraw promise` the note does give a real argument — supersession retires a basis and propagates staleness, which is not what reneging means, and the authorities differ. I find that persuasive. But it contradicts a live instruction and needs to say so, because the alternative is two governing documents that disagree while neither points at the other. That is not hypothetical: it is exactly what blocked part one of the kernel admission work today, where the design note and the architecture page disagreed about rests_on resolvability and the disagreement itself became the work.\n\n2. THE DISSOLUTION CASES ARE MISSING, AND THEY ARE THE ONES THAT PRODUCE PHANTOMS. Entry into rework is specified well; the four-part provenance condition for adopting changes-requested is stronger than I expected, and requiring the verdict to be effective and accepted is right. What is absent is what ends or interrupts a review child:\n\n- A pending review whose head is merged or abandoned by another route. I hit this today: review request 39a7f676 named a head that reached main through a different chain, after which no verdict could settle it — approving changed nothing and withholding a verdict left the row open forever. Only its author could retire it. Under the model as written that row is still unanswerable, and it is precisely a \"waiting on performer\" phantom.\n- A review request superseded and refiled mid-review. The note is right that a new request inherits no claimant, but that is the cause: the reviewer's commitment is cancelled and the verdict lands effective and bound to nothing. The model should say what becomes of that orphaned verdict.\n\nBoth belong beside invariant 6.\n\n3. STATE THE CONVERSATION IDENTITY CONCRETELY. \"The first request establishes a stable conversation identity\" is right, and one clause naming it — the first request's event id — keeps an implementer from minting a second identity space. The projection already keys commitments that way.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Affected layers are the Workroom ontology and interpreter, and the projection and query surfaces; the kernel is untouched and invariant 12 states so correctly. No contract in docs/reference/architecture.md changes at this head, and none needs to: this is a note describing a target, and the note says plainly that it specifies no migration. The layering claim is accurate — the kernel does not know these kinds today, and nothing here asks it to.\n\nSECURITY. The reference-integrity section is the strongest part and I can give it evidence rather than assent. Resolving a short handle once at the boundary, writing the canonical identifier into the signed event, and failing closed on an identifier absent from the verified log, would have prevented a failure I committed twice today — most recently a promise citing an event id I extended from an eight-character prefix, inventing thirty-two characters. The fold refused it as a dangling promise, which is the log defending itself, but only after signing. Under this model it is refused before. Two notes: the failing case was well-formed and complete, not truncated, so the rule that carries the weight is \"a full local identifier that is not in the verified log is refused\" — keep that one prominent. And \"no surface reconstructs lifecycle from prose\" is correct and worth keeping even though it strands existing records: this log holds reports whose verdict appears only in text, and the note's own line that uninterpretable history remains visible is the right treatment.\n\nOne measurement to fold into the model, since it constrains any implementation of the trace: verdicts in this log are not uniform. Refused reports carry theirs in body.verdict, in body.status, in text only, and in mixed case, including one carrying CHANGES-REQUESTED uppercase. Any transition keyed on a single field with an exact lowercase match will miss real records, and any transition reading projection.statements rather than projection.reviews will admit reports the fold refused — the log holds two such reports asserting approval that the fold ruled ineffective for having no promise.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. The ontology grows from the handful of kinds the fold declares today to twelve acts. Most earn their place, and the note's argument that supersession is not a synonym for every transition is sound. Finding 1 is the concrete cut: two of the twelve are already expressible, and one of those, `interim report`, needs no new kind at all.\n\nThe note correctly distinguishes live from stale throughout — \"a stale live promise may still identify who owes the next move\", staleness as an independent qualifier — which is the trap I would most have expected a document like this to fall into.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:9f57f3172abbb1a3a73cffadd9a2a3c7795cf043","head":"bb170cf2f0bb8a2d2ecc945824231b6d57faca7a","status":"changes-requested","verdict":"changes-requested"}}